<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bohemond I of Antioch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1054–<a href="/years/1111">1111</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Bohemond of Taranto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bohemond I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> was a Norman leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a>. Given Taranto in 1088, he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> and in 1098 secured Antioch for himself, founding the Principality of Antioch. Captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> in 1100 and ransomed in 1103, he later attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> but had to accept the Treaty of Devol in 1108.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bohemond-i-of-antioch.jpg" alt="Bohemond I of Antioch" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maître de Fauvel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bohemond_I_of_Antioch_lib.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bohemond was baptised Marc and nicknamed after a giant of legend. His parents, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> and Alberada of Buonalbergo, were related within the degrees forbidden by canon law, and Guiscard repudiated Alberada for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichelgaita">Sikelgaita</a>, sister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> prince of Salerno, which made Bohemond a bastard. His mother arranged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> education for him, and he was multilingual, speaking Norman and Greek. He commanded the advance guard of his father's invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 1081, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Valona</a>, and fought on the left flank at the victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a> on 18 October 1081.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Guiscard died on 17 July 1085, Bohemond contested the succession of his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-borsa">Roger Borsa</a>, and under a compromise received Taranto, Oria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otranto">Otranto</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>, though the war was renewed in 1087. In 1097 crusaders passing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> drew him east. He brought a Norman army of about 500 knights and some thousands of infantry, took the oath of homage to Alexios at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in April 1097, and served as a go-between with the other leaders. At the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> he was regarded as its actual leader. A commander of the wall, Firouz, let his troops in during May 1098, and he is credited with the plan that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerbogha">Kerbogha</a>'s relief army.</p><p>Bohemond kept the city, arguing that Alexios had failed to come to the crusaders' aid, and Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> gave up his claim in January 1099. In August 1100 he was ambushed by Turks near Melitene and held at Neo-Caesarea until 1103, when a ransom of 130,000 dinars was paid to Gazi Gümüshtigin. After a defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in late 1104 to raise troops, married Constance, daughter of Philip I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and gathered an army of 34,000 men. With it he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a> in 1107–1108, but Alexios blockaded his camp.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bohemond had to submit in 1108 to the Treaty of Deabolis, becoming Alexios's vassal with the title sebastos, promising to cede disputed territory and to admit a Greek patriarch to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. He never returned to Antioch, which stayed in Norman hands under his nephew Tancred, and died in March 1111, being buried at Canosa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. His son by Constance was Bohemond II of Antioch. The anonymous Gesta Francorum was written by one of his followers, and the Byzantine princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-komnene">Anna Komnene</a>'s Alexiad is a main source for his life; she wrote that the sight of him inspired admiration and the mention of his name terror.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bohemond I of Antioch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hauteville brothers Robert Guiscard and Roger I conquered southern Italy and Sicily between the 1040s and 1091, Roger II was crowned King of Sicily in 1130, and <b>Bohemond of Taranto</b> founded the Principality of Antioch during the First Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Crusaders besieged Antioch, <b>Bohemond of Taranto</b> gathered all the remaining horses and marched by night against a relief army led by Radwan, the Seljuq ruler of Aleppo.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-9">9 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A day after forcing the Iron Bridge over the Orontes, the First Crusade reached Antioch, <b>Bohemond of Taranto</b> leading the vanguard.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-21">21 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1100 Danishmend Gazi captured <b>Bohemond of Antioch</b>, and his son Gazi Gumushtigin was for a time the strongest ruler in Anatolia, receiving the title malik from the caliph.</blockquote><a href="/state/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bohemond of Taranto</b>, a Norman from southern Italy, kept the city for himself despite promises to return it to Byzantium, founding the principality.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Principality of Antioch</a></li><li><blockquote>After Robert Guiscard's death the Norman inheritance was divided, and in 1088 his eldest son <b>Bohemond</b> received Taranto and much of the heel of Italy as compensation for losing the Duchy of Apulia and Calabria to his half-brother Roger Borsa.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-taranto">Principality of Taranto</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Marco Argentano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canusium">Canosa di Puglia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.55,16.12&amp;year=1091" aria-label="San Marco Argentano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4948 747 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bohemond I of Antioch"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5398 1017h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5398 1017h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.55,16.12&amp;year=1091">What was here in 1091? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/robert-guiscard.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Robert Guiscard</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; c. 1015–1085</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Seignors, take heed all of you, for we are pilgrims of God. We ought, therefore, to be better and more humble than before. Do not plunder this land, since it belongs to Christians…&#8221;</p><footer>Words to his army on its march through the Balkans, 1096 (Gesta Francorum) &#183; tr. August C. Krey, 1921 &#183; as the anonymous Gesta Francorum, written by a knight in his following about 1100, records them &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/gesta-cde.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
